Date: Mon, 1 Feb 93 11:54 EST
From: kqb@whscad1.att.com
Subject: SOC/TECH: Viral Assault

This is the time of year when many people take advantage of the opportunity
to cause widespread economic devastation, simply by showing up for work,
when they should instead stay at home and get over their flu.  They then
cough, hack, and spread their disease to everyone within infection-distance
so that other people will, in turn, be able to come to work sick and infect
those who haven't yet gotten hit.  And there is no liability for the
perpetrators.  Somehow, people in our culture seem to be as resigned to
catching respiratory ailments during the winter as they are to eventually
dying.  "It's fate.  Everybody gets it."

Why is it that there are penalties for theft and physical assault, but,
in practice, none for the common variety of viral assault we experience
every year?  I think that it is largely because there is no way to
conclusively pinpoint the perpetrator.  Sure, it could have been Frank,
who valiantly came to work to complete the XYZ project by deadline even
though he was only half-conscious because of his flu.  But it could have
been someone on the bus or subway.  Or some animal.  Who knows?
What we have is a tragedy of the commons.  In this case, though, the
commons is a common pool of disease, not a common pool of resources.

Here is a situation where a modest advance in technology could change
our social mores, I think.  Suppose that we had a detector, at each
entrance to a building, that conveniently, automatically, and
non-invasively performed a "breath-alyzer" test on everyone to see if
they are expelling a prodigious amount of flu viruses. **  Anyone caught
harboring dangerous, infectious organisms would not be allowed on the
property.  Violation of privacy?  Perhaps.  But shouldn't an employer
be able to determine if someone is likely to spread disease throughout
the company and cost it a *lot* of money from sick leave?  My guess is
that once "infectiousness" of people is readily measured, our
consciousness about who causes the spread of disease will be heightened,
and the majority of people no longer will passively accept the annual
ritual of suffering from these diseases.  There will be more accountability
for such mass infection because we no longer will have such a "tragedy
of the commons."

Open Questions:
  Do Libertarians violate their non-agression agreement when committing
    such viral assault?
  What should we do about people who have compromised immune systems,
    and become large-scale breeding factories for dangerous,
    opportunistic, and easily-transmissible diseases?  (For example,
    people with AIDS do not spread AIDS readily, but they may spread
    other diseases they acquired, and mass-produced, because of their
    compromised immune systems.)  Before getting too gung-ho about this,
    though, consider that in Nazi Germany, the Jews were considered to be
    breeding grounds for horrible, vile diseases, too.  This could easily
    become a witch hunt.
                                              Kevin Q. Brown
                                              kqb@whscad1.att.com

** There certainly are other means of infection than airborne expulsion
   of viruses.  That's good news, though, because ViralGuard, Inc., and
   its competitors, can sell more kinds of detection units for those other
   modes of infection, too.

