Date: Mon, 1 Feb 93 12:16:44 CST
From: eder@hsvaic.boeing.com (Dani Eder)
Subject: the free market

Re: local road construction

The street in front of my house was built by the developer, Mr.
Bill Walker (that's why it's called Bill Walker Road).  It's
1/2 mile long and 25 feet wide.  He gave the right of way to the
county, and gets his investment back by selling 1.5 and 3 acre
lots in what was originally a farm.

If there were a way to get a property tax reduction for privately
held roads (there isn't), then he could quite easily have set up
a road maintenancec covenant when recording the subdivision plat.
This would require the property owners on the street to share the
maintenance costs according to some simple rule (like 3 acre lots
pay twice as much as 1.5 acre lots, or something like that).

The way the tax collection runs now, there is no savings to doing
so, so there is no point in setting up the association, but the
legal mechanisms already exist.  There are lots of property
developments where the owners all must belong to the association,
and lots of people willingly live under such rules.

My previous residence was at a large apartment complex on a lake.
The apartment complex and the single family homes on the other
side of the lake all had to belong to the community association.
The association paid for the maintenence of the dam that created
the lake, the boat ramp, and the community clubhouse (gym,
pool, tennis court, hall for parties).  The apartment complex
built their fee into the rent, and you could use the facilities
(like fishing in the lake).  It all seemed to work fairly well,
and if you didn't like the arangement, you didn't have to
live there.

Dani Eder

