Date: Mon, 1 Feb 1993 19:43:06 +0100 (MET)
From: ggoebel@sun1.ruf.uni-freiburg.de (Garrett Goebel)
Subject: ECON/PHIL: Selfish Socialism

To start, Perry, in reference to some wise words Tim May once said 
to me... I will write about and debate anything I please.  You do not
speak for the interests of the list as a whole, and if your mind is closed
to the subject, delete it.  On the other hand, the comments of Hal, Krzys',
Tim Starr, Dale, and Rob Hanson show that they are at least interested enough
to comment on it... if not a complete brainstorming session as in Tim's
case.  I do invite you to shred any argument I might put forth.

To continue, I whole heartedly agree with Krzys' that socialism is a 
modern tool for enslavement, but I believe I differ with regards to who
is being enslaved.  I believe that used properly, socialism is a tool
that the capitalist uses to enslave those who would revolt, into the
system of the market economy.

I must admit at this point that I am probably not using socialism in the 
original sense of the term, as Tim Starr points out.  It is mainly as a
redistribution of "minimal opportunity" that I am using socialism, not as
Tim's definition... which sounds more of communism to me.  By "minimal
opportunity" I refer to the amount of redistribution necessary to stem
off revolts/strikes/anti-capitalist votes/etc.  And by redistribution I
do not only mean funds, but access to knowledge, ie. education and 
opportunities for self-improvement.
  
To this end, I would stress Tim's point about changes in socialism in a
different way.  Just because there are no current renowned political
philosopher's championing socialism doesn't mean it will die out.  We
should start with the reality that the wealthiest most post-industrial
economies are very much mixed economies.  Theories stem from reality,
not the other way around.  Yes, the original term as related to property
rights is dying a quick and speedy death rightly so... but socialism IS
transforming itself... and I'm not so sure that redistribution is nearing
it's end. 

Here's a quote with a source to raise Perry's hackles:

Richard McKenzie a speaker at last years Ludwig von Mises Lecture Series
at Hillsdale College, Hillsdale Michigan stated:

    "However, in the Marxian analysis there remains an important element of 
   truth:  political institutions are grounded, to a meaningful degree, in
   technology."

This was preceeded by a quote from Marx himself:
   
    "In acquiring new productive forces men change their modes of production,
   and in changing their mode of production, their manner of gaining a living,
   they change all their institutions."

The reason why I bring this up is that a transformation in the practice
of socialism is more likily to be a result of the transformations of 
technology to society than to the death of offshoot theories of Lenin 
and Marx.  Better put, I don't think and I hope we are not going towards
a "therapy" state.  Negative prejudices about socialism aside, preventative
socalism like the compromise dealings that Rob wants to get by better 
guaging, "the actual negotiating value of the threat to revolt" to help 
stem off a revolt, are better than cleaning up the mess of revolt/strikes
afterward.

The "free give-aways" of socialism are not free at all.  It is a price that
capitalists pay to make the non-capitalists play by their rules.  Socialism,
in the since of redistributing "minimal opportunity" is the cost of
capitalism.  As technology transforms society, the was that price is paid
will change.

Today we are seeing a wealth of paradoxes... expanding the role of government,
and privitization and deregulation of industries, globalization of corps
and world governing organizations vs. increased regionalization, nationalism,
and ethnic strife.  No longer the West vs. the East and the North vs. the
South... but the Fast economies vs. the Slow economies... all of this is
occuring because technology is making knowledge and knowledge about 
knowledge the strongest source of power in the upcoming century.  (Read
Powershift by Alvin Toffler if you haven't already).

What this means to me, is that the governments and corporations of the 
future won't just tax your dollars, but your knowledge... luckily taxing
knowledge doesn't remove your possession of it.  What I mean, is that 
socialism will incorporate redistributing opportunity to the poor in the
form of money for subsistence and money and knowledge for continuing
education and retraining for the underpaid and unemployed, ie. socialism
survives and grows as countries expand in the attempt to raise competitiveness
through raising educational standards, and increased incentives for research.

This is the "socialism" that I believe is inevitable.  In the near future,
I am sure that governments will organize this dispersal of socialism... but
there is no reason why the private sector couldn't also overlap and eventually
replace an inefficient bureaucracy.  Even Perry admits that education would
be useful for decreasing the inefficiencies (though he'd probably never admit
that government sponsered education is good).  Eventually, it is hoped that
private education could take the place of government education, but Dale's
point that even education about the cost and benifits of various measures
won't work if the UAW figures out (correctly) that NAFTA is going to hurt
them and activily work against it, is well put (long sentence).

Still, redistribution of "minimal opportunity" is something that I see
as being both a necessary buffer, and unavoidable... don't you?

ggoebel


