Subject: ECON: Zero-Sum Games

I wrote:
> So are real financial markets, such as equities, futures, betting,
> etc., zero-sum?  It seems clear to me now that they are not zero sum 
> exactly to the extent that they influence behaviour *outside* those 
> markets. 

Hal Finney responded:
>I think this overlooks the payment of dividends.  Different stocks pay
>at different rates, and my strategies include the option of staying
>out of the market entirely, so I would not suppose that they can be
>scaled out of the equation.  Dividends, at least in some theories, are
>what actually give stocks their value, ...
>Dividends aren't paid for futures, so this seems like a difference.

Imagine we had a poker game where the pot gave out a small dividend to
those still in play - would this make poker less zero-sum?  Futures
could similarly offer dividends if there was any point to doing so.
Conversely, if tax laws were kind, companies could just buy back stock
instead of offering dividends.  When you can buy companies whole,
taking them private, dividends are not the only fundamental value of a
company.

>In a trade, I give up commodity A and get B, while my partner does
>the opposite.  Evidentally this represents an increase in utility for
>both of us, else we wouldn't trade. 

But of course, I trade you my apple for your orange because I like
oranges better today.  But for most of us the only value in owning
stock is the future monetary income it provides.  So there are only
two main reasons we might each value stocks differently 1) different
risk preferences 2) different beliefs.  Same as in poker.

Robin Hanson

