Subject: ECON: Are Medicare Expenditures Too High?

   From: KMOSTA01@ulkyvx02.louisville.edu

   Unfortunately, Dale is incorrect in assuming that countries with lowest
   medical inflation rate are those with fully nationalized health care.
   Germany has the lowest medical inflation rate, their system is a peculiar
   mixture of private insurance and government involvement, but there may
   be less government involvement in Germany overall than in the U.S.

Yes, but you're omitting an essential point of the system: Health care
fees are set for each geographical area ("lander", provinces, I think)
by a negotiation between associations of providers and associations of
insurers.  Thus, although the provision of health care is not
nationalized, the price structure (including which services will be
available) is *totally* controlled by the government.  (This is unlike
the British system, where there is an uncontrolled private health care
system that provides care that the nationalized system does not.)
The prices are controlled so that expenditures remain at 6% of GDP.

Whether this is more or less government involvement than in the US, I
can't really say.  It is interesting that it places the point of
involvement exactly where people care -- at the price list, rather
than trying to manipulate other aspects of the system to produce price
results that are deemed "desirable".

It also results in German doctors making about 1/2 of what US doctors
make.  But then, we don't allow them to immigrate, so the world doctor
market can be kept out of equilibrium...

Dale

