Date: Mon, 1 Feb 93 17:11:07 EST
From: pmetzger@shearson.com (Perry E. Metzger)
Subject: recovering encrypted records

> From: drw@bourbaki.mit.edu
> 
>    From: kqb@whscad1.att.com
> 
>    Aren't there some schemes where any M out of N "keys" are
>    [necessary and] sufficient to generate the real key?
> 
> The mathematicians and CS types have constructed systems like this;
> I've seen references to papers on the subject.  Try posting to
> sci.crypt for references.

No need; the concept is trivial. If you want to slice a secret into M bits
such that a quorum of N parts is needed to reconstruct the secret,
where N < M, you can do the following (due to Shamir):

Set up an Nth degree polynomial (over an integer field Z_p, usually, but
that doesn't matter to the concept) such that the coefficients of the
polynomial contain your secret. Then distribute M points along the polynomial.
To reconstruct the polynomial (and thus its coefficients and thus the
secret) requires N points. (I may have a fencepost error in there, but
you get the idea if you know a bit of math.)

This idea is described in Shamir's paper "How to share a secret". If you
think about it, you should be able to prove to yourself that it is impossible
to determine the coefficents of the polynomial with less than the required
number of points.

